1
00:00:01,010 --> 00:00:08,630
Hi, my name's Lauren. You might know me as poteto, and I work on Grok Bot at SpaceX AI.

2
00:00:09,470 --> 00:00:17,230
So last month, I did something pretty crazy. I shipped 2,000 pull requests to production.

3
00:00:18,650 --> 00:00:30,850
A lot of how I'm able to do this is through trust, and I think a lot about trust in terms of how I can trust my agents to produce high-quality work,

4
00:00:31,010 --> 00:00:33,250
even when I'm not there.

5
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:36,310
And my argument and thesis for this talk today

6
00:00:36,310 --> 00:00:41,570
is that if you set up your environment for your agents

7
00:00:41,570 --> 00:00:44,010
really, really well, you can end up with something

8
00:00:44,010 --> 00:00:49,490
that looks more like a personal or even team software factory,

9
00:00:49,490 --> 00:00:52,810
where you're producing very high quality code

10
00:00:52,810 --> 00:00:55,930
at much greater rates than before.

11
00:00:57,210 --> 00:01:00,510
But I'm not a fan of the term software factory.

12
00:01:00,510 --> 00:01:11,910
I like the analogy of a Michelin Kitchen better, where, you know, as technologists, we're not really producing, we're not mass-producing a product on an assembly line.

13
00:01:12,550 --> 00:01:21,190
But the work that we do looks very creative, it's very, you know, it's the act of building a product and it's art in some sense.

14
00:01:21,730 --> 00:01:30,170
So, you know, even though with agents, we're not cooking the individual components that go into the product anymore,

15
00:01:30,510 --> 00:01:39,310
we're still responsible for the final outcome and thinking about our kitchen setup, right?

16
00:01:39,310 --> 00:01:57,990
Because depending on how you set up, your line cooks, your sous chefs, you know, the kind of equipment they have, the kind of training they have, you know, dishwashers and the ratio, I guess, of line cooks to dishwashers, all of these ingredients go into making the final product.

17
00:01:57,990 --> 00:02:02,190
and I think this analogy is really apt.

18
00:02:02,950 --> 00:02:09,130
So I wanted to talk a little bit about a story before we begin,

19
00:02:09,970 --> 00:02:13,390
where six months ago, when I first joined Cursor,

20
00:02:13,590 --> 00:02:15,410
before we were a part of SpaceX AI,

21
00:02:16,050 --> 00:02:20,950
I obviously had, you know, no agent skills to use.

22
00:02:21,350 --> 00:02:22,550
I just joined the company.

23
00:02:22,550 --> 00:02:26,950
It was a fresh code base and a fresh product that I was working on.

24
00:02:27,990 --> 00:02:37,190
So at the time, Cursor was building the replacement for the Cursor IDE, which is the new agent's window.

25
00:02:38,230 --> 00:02:44,030
And before I joined, the Cursor agent window had quite a lot of performance issues.

26
00:02:44,870 --> 00:02:48,530
And my manager at the time asked if I was able to help them.

27
00:02:49,570 --> 00:02:56,190
And since I had spent some time on the React team before I joined Cursor, it seemed like a good fit.

28
00:02:56,190 --> 00:03:03,150
But when I first started, I quickly realized how manual this process was.

29
00:03:04,050 --> 00:03:23,310
Obviously, I have done performance work before, but at the rate at which pull requests were being landed, it was just, it felt almost an insurmountable wall of pull requests that just kept coming in, and I had no idea whether or not the performance of the app would be regressing, right?

30
00:03:23,310 --> 00:03:36,810
So a lot of my early time on the cursor team was spent looking at Chrome DevTools and doing performance traces and taking heap snapshots.

31
00:03:37,590 --> 00:03:40,610
And it was extremely, extremely manual.

32
00:03:41,850 --> 00:03:43,670
It was so manual.

33
00:03:43,970 --> 00:03:50,950
It got to the point where I just got really frustrated and started to think about, you know, wait, we have agents.

34
00:03:51,070 --> 00:03:51,670
What am I doing?

35
00:03:51,670 --> 00:04:12,590
And so I started thinking about verification skills, where, you know, what if my agent could actually run the application itself and take the traces for me, understand the traces and find the hot spots, and basically hill climb, you know, to a better performance in our application automatically.

36
00:04:14,150 --> 00:04:21,650
And throughout the last six months of being a cursor and SpaceX AI, you can really see that my product,

37
00:04:21,670 --> 00:04:29,510
it really has skyrocketed. And, you know, I never set out to ship 2,000 pull requests a month.

38
00:04:29,690 --> 00:04:36,450
That was not a goal of mine at all. But I realized that all of the skills, all of the tools and

39
00:04:36,450 --> 00:04:43,050
code-based changes I were making laddered up to this idea of trust. You know, I didn't know it at

40
00:04:43,050 --> 00:04:50,390
the time, but I had this thought in the back of my head, which was, you know, I am the bottleneck

41
00:04:50,390 --> 00:05:04,170
and I need to be able to take all of the knowledge that I have as an engineer and impart them into my team of agents so that I didn't need to be the blocker for everything.

42
00:05:04,670 --> 00:05:09,510
And you can clearly see that it's paid off.

43
00:05:11,250 --> 00:05:20,370
So I think it really comes down to trust, but how exactly do you build that trust?

44
00:05:20,390 --> 00:05:27,010
and where do you start if you are, you know, wherever you are in your journey of using agents?

45
00:05:27,770 --> 00:05:34,130
So when I started, obviously, I was in this category, you know, in the one to one to five range,

46
00:05:34,970 --> 00:05:41,890
where you still feel like you have to babysit every chat and conversation,

47
00:05:42,670 --> 00:05:48,470
and you're just constantly course correcting, you know, you're intervening, you're correcting your agent

48
00:05:48,470 --> 00:05:50,410
so that it does the right thing.

49
00:05:51,410 --> 00:05:55,450
And if you're not there, basically nothing happens,

50
00:05:56,010 --> 00:05:57,950
and the agents do the wrong thing.

51
00:05:59,210 --> 00:06:02,730
And I would actually argue that this phase of, you know,

52
00:06:02,770 --> 00:06:05,190
using agents is actually the hardest to get out of,

53
00:06:05,610 --> 00:06:10,050
because it's not always very clear how exactly you get out of it.

54
00:06:11,090 --> 00:06:13,870
And again, it just comes down to trust.

55
00:06:15,030 --> 00:06:18,390
You know, the reason you're unable to go from,

56
00:06:18,470 --> 00:06:25,490
one or one to five agents to something more like a hundred is because you don't have trust in your

57
00:06:25,490 --> 00:06:30,810
agents work yet. So if you don't have trust and you try to spawn a hundred sub-agents or cloud

58
00:06:30,810 --> 00:06:36,070
agents, you're going to quickly find that you're just going to get a ton of slop pull requests

59
00:06:36,070 --> 00:06:41,210
and, you know, a bunch of regressions and a bunch of bugs ship and no one's going to be very

60
00:06:41,210 --> 00:06:49,220
happy with them. So that begs to question, how do you trust your agents more?

61
00:06:49,840 --> 00:07:04,780
So for me, it came, it really started when I, like I said, I meant I joined the cursor team, and I was starting to work on performance, where I realized the need for verification.

62
00:07:05,700 --> 00:07:19,780
So when I say verification, there are sort of levels to that because, you know, on the, I guess, the lower end of the scale for verification, you have things like the verification.

63
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:26,800
skills that I talk about where you teach your agent how to run your application and use

64
00:07:26,800 --> 00:07:32,560
things like the Chrome DevTools protocol or whatever other protocol that you have for debugging

65
00:07:32,560 --> 00:07:38,960
and teach them how to you know debug the application take performance traces

66
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:46,800
take heap snapshots and so on and then on the opposite end of spectrum of the

67
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:53,480
spectrum, which is much, much harder and still very much an open question, is like more formal

68
00:07:53,480 --> 00:08:00,660
verification, where, you know, maybe you rely on formal methods or, you know, languages like

69
00:08:00,660 --> 00:08:08,160
lean or TLA+ to do, you know, verification so that you can check that, you know, your

70
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:15,700
business level or business logic invariants are, you know, are always true.

71
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:23,800
and that you can formally verify that your application is always in a correct state.

72
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:32,800
But I would say that even if you don't have the ability to run formal methods and very few people really do,

73
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:36,800
that with verification skills you can get very far.

74
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:42,800
So when I joined the cursor and started to work on the cursor agent window,

75
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:46,300
the first skill that I built was the skill called control glass,

76
00:08:46,300 --> 00:08:54,960
which is a verification skill that teaches the agent how to run the application and take traces, like I mentioned,

77
00:08:55,460 --> 00:08:59,080
and it does that through the Chrome DevTools Protocol.

78
00:09:01,350 --> 00:09:07,410
Something interesting about that skill is, and I kind of iterated my way to this,

79
00:09:07,830 --> 00:09:17,310
it didn't start out this way, but the control or verification skill really has two components to it.

80
00:09:17,550 --> 00:09:47,110
The first part is obviously a CLI, so you want your agents to be able to reproducibly be able to run the application and collect traces and collect evidence that, you know, empirical evidence, that, you know, code is working, that your performance bar is being met, and rather than have your agents create scripts every time, you know, that can differ between agents.

81
00:09:47,110 --> 00:09:54,530
agent sessions, you can actually create a CLI that's within the skill directory,

82
00:09:54,810 --> 00:10:00,890
and then your agents will just use that every time. And then, of course, you need to actually

83
00:10:00,890 --> 00:10:05,930
invest in it and make it good so that it can handle all sorts of different use cases

84
00:10:05,930 --> 00:10:13,870
and be able to, you know, run the application correctly.

85
00:10:13,870 --> 00:10:20,910
Another thing that's also really important is this idea of a feature map.

86
00:10:22,030 --> 00:10:30,030
So a feature map really is something that I kind of coined, I guess, where, you know, we started

87
00:10:30,030 --> 00:10:36,030
using these control skills within cursor. Then we quickly realized that, you know, a Slack report

88
00:10:36,030 --> 00:10:42,190
would come in and a user would post a very vague screenshot, right? Like a very small slice of the

89
00:10:42,190 --> 00:10:48,130
UI and just like three question marks. And the agents using our control skills are just no idea.

90
00:10:48,130 --> 00:11:02,030
Like it could run the application, but it was just guessing at what exactly the user meant. And so I had this idea to create something called a feature map, which is, I guess, kind of inspired by like a site map.

91
00:11:02,950 --> 00:11:12,170
It essentially is a form of materialized memory. You know, like how exactly does your application work? What features

92
00:11:12,190 --> 00:11:19,070
does it have, how does a user reach it, you know, in terms of keyboard shortcuts or what DOM

93
00:11:19,070 --> 00:11:24,270
elements to click on, you know, things like that, and what all the different features do.

94
00:11:26,110 --> 00:11:33,630
And they are these, this feature map is stored in the skill itself in the code base as part

95
00:11:33,630 --> 00:11:39,390
of the skill directory. And we have an automation that maintains this feature map as well.

96
00:11:39,390 --> 00:12:01,870
But when we combine the CLI and the feature map, we quickly realized that this combination was very, very powerful because now agents could not only, you know, reproducibly control the application and take traces, but it could also understand requests that came in from internal users as well as external users.

97
00:12:01,870 --> 00:12:17,470
And so we very quickly realized that the control verification skills were so useful that they've become more or less critical infrastructure for our team, and we constantly maintain it.

98
00:12:18,110 --> 00:12:31,850
But the ability for an agent to verify its own work is extremely powerful, and we have spent a lot of time on this skill, and it's very, very powerful for building that trust.

99
00:12:31,870 --> 00:12:41,370
trust. In addition to verification, of course, you want, because verification is really about

100
00:12:41,370 --> 00:12:48,370
correctness. Correctness to me is really about, does the thing, that's the feature or code

101
00:12:48,370 --> 00:12:53,930
do the thing that you want it to do, right? Like, does the, you know, the checkout button, does it

102
00:12:53,930 --> 00:13:00,070
actually check out the card? Verification is really important for that because you can get

103
00:13:00,070 --> 00:13:05,750
empirical evidence that this feature actually works. But it doesn't tell you much about,

104
00:13:05,750 --> 00:13:13,150
you know, the performance or the code quality of that feature. And that's where you start thinking

105
00:13:13,150 --> 00:13:22,590
about skills that teach agents to work like real software engineers. So I've built a plugin called

106
00:13:22,590 --> 00:13:29,910
pstack. I'm not going to talk about the plugin too much today, but a lot of inspiration

107
00:13:29,910 --> 00:13:37,650
for that plugin, which is a collection of skills that I've created, are inspired by the kind of

108
00:13:37,650 --> 00:13:45,370
workflows that I personally have used in my time doing software engineering for all sorts of

109
00:13:45,370 --> 00:13:52,750
different types of tasks. So debugging, you know, feature development, prototyping, there's a whole

110
00:13:52,750 --> 00:13:59,750
bunch of different playbooks and skills that ship in pstack that teach your agents how to write code

111
00:13:59,750 --> 00:14:01,210
the way that you want them to.

112
00:14:02,090 --> 00:14:07,130
And, you know, this is where, you know, the more experienced engineers on your team can really

113
00:14:07,130 --> 00:14:16,030
contribute to set up a team repository of skills that just make your agents a lot smarter.

114
00:14:17,870 --> 00:14:24,930
And when you combine those skills with verification skills, then you're able to get the point

115
00:14:24,930 --> 00:14:29,730
where your agents are able to not just verify that the work that they're doing is correct.

116
00:14:29,750 --> 00:14:31,950
but that is also high quality.

117
00:14:32,830 --> 00:14:35,050
And again, because of verification,

118
00:14:35,730 --> 00:14:39,010
you can collect real performance metrics,

119
00:14:39,190 --> 00:14:42,150
you can get real numbers and statistics and telemetry

120
00:14:42,150 --> 00:14:47,370
on the performance of the application.

121
00:14:49,930 --> 00:14:53,490
So I think that's a really important part to invest in.

122
00:14:55,690 --> 00:14:57,470
Another thing I think is really important

123
00:14:57,470 --> 00:14:59,730
is refactoring and rewriting

124
00:14:59,750 --> 00:15:02,730
your architector to be more agent-friendly.

125
00:15:04,110 --> 00:15:11,510
And I almost want to say that this is one of the most important things you can do as a software

126
00:15:11,510 --> 00:15:17,550
engineering team, because if you really truly believe that agents are going to be writing

127
00:15:17,550 --> 00:15:23,630
all the code in the future, then we need to design our code bases so that they do the right

128
00:15:23,630 --> 00:15:24,690
thing by default.

129
00:15:25,470 --> 00:15:29,350
And you'll find that there's this, I almost want to say like a,

130
00:15:29,750 --> 00:15:37,430
a scale or continuum between, you know, these different pieces of building trust in your agents,

131
00:15:38,390 --> 00:15:44,070
where on, you know, I have like five of these points here, where, uh,

132
00:15:45,590 --> 00:15:52,630
the code base is really like the best form of memory because agents love to extend existing

133
00:15:52,630 --> 00:15:59,590
patterns that they see. Um, and, you know, uh, I think this is just the nature of how LLMs work.

134
00:15:59,750 --> 00:16:07,750
where they're more likely to use whatever it is in their context window to make changes.

135
00:16:07,750 --> 00:16:14,750
And of course the files that the agents read and opens are part of its context window,

136
00:16:14,750 --> 00:16:18,750
and therefore the code base is a really important part of that.

137
00:16:18,750 --> 00:16:24,750
Because, you know, agents aren't going to just refactor your code in every single PR.

138
00:16:24,750 --> 00:16:27,750
They're going to just look at what's already there and just extend.

139
00:16:27,750 --> 00:16:53,070
The next level I think is about static analysis, where you have linters, you have compiler diagnostics, you have continuous integration, and these are guidelines and constraints that you can enforce in your code base so that whenever you correct your agent, you find that, you know, they just keep making the same mistake.

140
00:16:53,070 --> 00:17:04,310
you can add those as lint rules or even better you can refactor your code base so that the mistake that the agent is making becomes categorically impossible

141
00:17:04,310 --> 00:17:23,050
and then a step above that right where and this is where we're trying to get more into less of like a hard constraint and enforcement and more into the realm of guidance you have things like rules you have Bugbot you have skills which

142
00:17:23,070 --> 00:17:28,670
you know, your agents will obviously sometimes and mostly use when they're doing their work,

143
00:17:28,810 --> 00:17:34,090
but there is also a chance that it might, for various reasons, you know, forget to read a rule

144
00:17:34,090 --> 00:17:39,430
or maybe the user that is piloting the agent, ignores them.

145
00:17:40,130 --> 00:17:48,390
So these aren't quite as enforceable, but also an important part of setting up your environment

146
00:17:48,390 --> 00:17:51,070
so that you can really trust what your agents are doing.

147
00:17:53,070 --> 00:17:58,990
And then finally you have a style guide, which is really only enforceable by humans in a code review.

148
00:17:59,430 --> 00:18:03,610
I guess you could put these in your rules and Bugbot and skills as well.

149
00:18:04,090 --> 00:18:10,570
But if you don't, then you have this big glaring hole in your review process,

150
00:18:10,570 --> 00:18:17,130
where now humans have to, you know, look at every single line that's being changed and remember the comment.

151
00:18:17,590 --> 00:18:22,290
And with the rate of pull requests that are coming in, it just becomes impossible.

152
00:18:23,070 --> 00:18:26,810
So I definitely wouldn't recommend, you know, relying only on the style guide.

153
00:18:26,810 --> 00:18:35,190
I think the style guide or, you know, like looking at human reviews, is a good place to start in terms of what's missing.

154
00:18:35,670 --> 00:18:53,050
But you should really invest the time to think about the other four parts, you know, your code base, making things categorically impossible through better data structures or algorithms, static analysis, and then, of course, you layer that with rules.

155
00:18:53,070 --> 00:18:54,970
and Bugbot and skills.

156
00:18:57,010 --> 00:18:58,770
On the code base front,

157
00:19:01,830 --> 00:19:03,530
and in the Grok Bot code base,

158
00:19:04,070 --> 00:19:06,290
we actually have invested into

159
00:19:06,290 --> 00:19:08,630
setting something up that we call

160
00:19:08,630 --> 00:19:12,070
Dune, which is our agent-friendly framework.

161
00:19:13,130 --> 00:19:16,930
So the inspiration for Dune really came about

162
00:19:16,930 --> 00:19:18,590
from a lot of the performance issues

163
00:19:18,590 --> 00:19:22,390
we were seeing in the cursor agent window.

164
00:19:23,430 --> 00:19:24,430
And so a lot of lessons

165
00:19:24,450 --> 00:19:33,330
came out of that exploration, but the key principle that we landed on is really that agents love

166
00:19:33,330 --> 00:19:41,870
taking shortcuts. So what if we designed a framework such that the shortcut, you know, the easy path

167
00:19:41,870 --> 00:19:48,890
is the right path for agents. And also one that would be a code base that is, you know,

168
00:19:48,890 --> 00:19:54,430
maybe pretty annoying for humans to work in because it's so locked down in terms of what you

169
00:19:54,450 --> 00:20:00,010
can do and what you can't do. But it actually creates the perfect environment for agents,

170
00:20:00,410 --> 00:20:05,730
especially ones that have very minimal context because, you know, not every contributor to your

171
00:20:05,730 --> 00:20:10,410
codebase is going to be an engineer anymore. You can have designers, you can have product

172
00:20:10,410 --> 00:20:16,990
managers, you can have CEOs, you know, going into the codebase and shipping features. So we want

173
00:20:16,990 --> 00:20:24,110
to really think a lot about how we invest and set up our codebases so that, you know, even

174
00:20:24,110 --> 00:20:30,030
agents that are piloted by busy people with not a lot of context can do a good job by default.

175
00:20:31,710 --> 00:20:39,310
And like I mentioned before, your codebase is really a form of memory for agents because they

176
00:20:39,310 --> 00:20:45,390
love to extend the existing patterns that they see. And the reverse is actually also true,

177
00:20:45,710 --> 00:20:51,630
right? You can invest a time to set up your code base in a way that things are, you know,

178
00:20:51,630 --> 00:20:53,870
bad patterns are categorically impossible.

179
00:20:54,110 --> 00:20:56,950
or you have lint rules that prevent them

180
00:20:56,950 --> 00:21:00,190
but the reverse is also true in the sense that

181
00:21:00,190 --> 00:21:03,650
if you have existing anti-patterns

182
00:21:03,650 --> 00:21:06,570
you'll actually find that these will spread

183
00:21:06,570 --> 00:21:08,070
kind of like a virus

184
00:21:08,070 --> 00:21:12,870
where you have like one small workaround

185
00:21:12,870 --> 00:21:15,530
or a comment that explains a workaround

186
00:21:15,530 --> 00:21:19,290
and you'll quickly find that agents just love to copy that

187
00:21:19,290 --> 00:21:23,110
and then in a matter of a few days or a few weeks

188
00:21:23,110 --> 00:21:25,990
you'll find that the workaround has spread everywhere

189
00:21:25,990 --> 00:21:31,910
and it's now becomes, it has become like a de facto pattern for all agents.

190
00:21:32,070 --> 00:21:34,410
And that's a really, really bad place to be in.

191
00:21:35,210 --> 00:21:38,310
And that goes back to what I was saying about why it's really important,

192
00:21:38,310 --> 00:21:41,230
you know, whenever you're correcting your agents,

193
00:21:41,430 --> 00:21:45,670
that you invest the time into thinking about code-based changes

194
00:21:45,670 --> 00:21:52,290
and static analysis and, of course, layering them with rules, good rules,

195
00:21:52,290 --> 00:22:02,610
and Bugbot and skills because of that reason and another analogy that I like in addition to the

196
00:22:02,610 --> 00:22:08,130
Michelin Kitchen is this idea that you know your codebase is kind of like a garden where you

197
00:22:08,130 --> 00:22:16,530
have workarounds you know that seem seemingly that seem kind of innocent at first but then because

198
00:22:16,530 --> 00:22:22,130
of the nature of agents you just copy that pattern over and over again and you quickly end up with

199
00:22:22,130 --> 00:22:29,550
a very, you know, vibe-coded codebase that is, you know, a pain in the butt to maintain

200
00:22:29,550 --> 00:22:36,450
